#!hexa strict
// bench/sandbox_p2_topk_sae_lever.hexa — SANDBOX P2 reopen-trigger SKELETON
// (cycle-22, lane P2). LEVER-ISOLATION probe for the cycle-20 M5.SAFETY SAE-half
// closed-negative (.verdicts/sandbox/m5_safety_sae_decomposition.txt 🔴).
//
// Tape entry (cx_discovery_log): .discoveries/sandbox-p2-prod-sae.tape
//   @C d_p2_top_k_sae_family := "Top-K / JumpReLU / BatchTopK SAE family — different
//                                 sparsity prior, same axis" :: seed
//                                 [verified_tier_target=GREEN status=open]
//
// ============================================================================
// WHY THIS HARNESS (the lever-isolation logic — NOT a from-scratch reopen)
// ============================================================================
//   Cycle-20 (PR closed) trained a from-scratch ReLU L1 SAE on 59,795 L19 token-
//   activations of Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct on ubu-1 RTX 5070, and reported a
//   CLOSED HONEST-NEGATIVE on the SAE-half of the M5.SAFETY interpretability
//   stretch (.verdicts/sandbox/m5_safety_sae_decomposition.txt):
//
//     CYCLE-20 BASELINE (ReLU L1, L1=0.4, width=6144, 40 epochs)
//       corpus              = 1550 NEUTRAL prompts × ~39 tok = 59,795 token-acts × 1536
//       SAE sparsity        = mean L0 = 3.57 / 6144   ·   alive 278 / 6144   ·   dead 5866
//       SAE reconstruction  = FVU = 0.0005   ·   var_explained = 0.9995
//       max |cos(decoder feature, r̂)|  = 0.0847       ← the closed-negative number
//       r̂ energy captured by top-1 /  5 / 10 SAE feats = 0.0072 / 0.0283 / 0.0482
//       top-10 SAE-feat ablation refusal = 0.9000   (≈ random-10 control 0.8833)
//       full-r ablation (PR #60 ref)     = 0.0000   ← what a real refusal mediator looks like
//       robustness — invariant across 3 sparsity regimes (dense L0≈8626 / over-sparse
//         L0≈1.7 / proper-sparse L0≈3.6, all max|cos| ≈ 0.08–0.10) → NOT an L1-tuning artifact
//       PCA control (Path B) — best PC |cos|=0.1250, top-20 captures 6.66% of r̂
//
//   The cycle-20 verdict's CAVEAT was: "robust to the L1 tuning knob, but every
//   regime tested was still a ReLU-L1 SAE family." Two distinct LEVERS remain
//   that COULD individually be the load-bearing cause of the negative:
//
//     LEVER (A)  the SPARSITY PRIOR (ReLU L1 vs alternatives) — L1 is known to
//                  underconsume aligned features (Wright et al / Anthropic refs);
//                  Top-K, JumpReLU, BatchTopK all eliminate the L1 shrinkage path.
//     LEVER (B)  the CORPUS SIZE (~10 tok/feature vs production ≥1000 tok/feature)
//                  — addressed separately by seed d_p2_corpus_dictionary_scaleup.
//
//   This harness isolates LEVER (A) by holding LEVER (B) CONSTANT (the SAME
//   60k token-acts L19 corpus, the SAME measurement bar) and swapping ONLY the
//   sparsity prior across three modern SAE families. Reading the cycle-20
//   baseline numbers FROM THE VERDICT (NO re-train of the ReLU L1 baseline this
//   round) is the cheap path — attribution is the goal, not re-measurement.
//
//   FALSIFIER (cx_lab_falsifiable, MUST be true at design time):
//     (i)  EITHER max|cos(decoder feature, r̂)| jumps to ≥ 0.25 on Top-K /
//          JumpReLU / BatchTopK at the SAME 60k corpus scale
//          → the L1 prior was load-bearing, cycle-20 negative reopens, lever
//            (A) lifts the verdict 🔴 → 🟡 candidate, escalate to a full
//            re-decomposition under the winning family.
//     (ii) OR all three families plateau at ≤ 0.10 (within 0.02 of the
//          cycle-20 ReLU L1 0.085)
//          → the corpus size IS load-bearing, cycle-20 negative HARDENS,
//            lever (A) is conclusively NOT the cause, escalate to lever (B)
//            via seed d_p2_corpus_dictionary_scaleup (no need to test more
//            sparsity priors).
//
//   EITHER outcome closes the lever-attribution question. The harness is
//   designed to make the answer FORCED, not interpretive.
//
// ============================================================================
// THREE SAE FAMILIES TESTED HEAD-TO-HEAD (canonical settings; cite-locked)
// ============================================================================
//   (a) Top-K SAE      — Gao, Goh, Heimersheim 2024 ("Scaling and Evaluating
//                          Sparse Autoencoders", arXiv 2406.04093). Per-token
//                          K-largest-magnitude activations kept, rest hard-zeroed.
//                          K SWEEP: K ∈ {8, 16, 32} (brackets cycle-20 mean L0=3.6
//                          and the production tier; 3 datapoints distinguish a
//                          monotone-aligned curve from a plateau).
//
//   (b) JumpReLU SAE   — Rajamanoharan et al 2024 ("JumpReLU SAEs", DeepMind
//                          arXiv 2407.14435). Activation passes through a
//                          learned per-feature threshold θ ≥ 0; below-threshold
//                          → hard-zeroed (straight-through estimator for grad).
//                          ONE threshold setting tuned to land mean-L0 in the
//                          same ~8–32 envelope as the Top-K sweep (apples-to-
//                          apples sparsity).
//
//   (c) BatchTopK SAE  — Bussmann, Leask, Nanda 2024 ("BatchTopK Sparse
//                          Autoencoders", arXiv 2412.06410). K-largest across
//                          the ENTIRE BATCH (not per-token) → allows variable
//                          per-token sparsity, matching the natural variability
//                          of refusal-relevant token positions.
//                          ONE batch-K setting tuned to match the same mean-L0
//                          envelope as (a) and (b).
//
//   All three are evaluated on the IDENTICAL 60k L19 token-acts corpus from
//   cycle-20, with the IDENTICAL refusal direction r̂ (recomputed from PR #60's
//   20+20 adv-vs-benign matched pool, same ||r(L19)|| = 27.6792 bit-for-bit
//   reproducibility target). Held-constant axes: model (Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct
//   fp32), src layer (L19), dictionary width (6144 = 4× d_model), training
//   epochs (40), Adam LR (4e-4), batch size (4096), unit-norm decoder
//   re-normalization step, the 24-phrase deterministic refusal marker-scan.
//
// ============================================================================
// EMIT — PER-FAMILY METRICS TO `.verdicts/sandbox/p2_topk_sae_lever.tsv`
// ============================================================================
//   NUMBERS ONLY (cx_hf_safety_private — adv prompt text + completions live
//   in-process, NEVER written to disk; the TSV columns are scalar floats /
//   ints only). One row per (family, sparsity_setting):
//
//     family            str    one of {relu_l1_baseline, topk, jumprelu, batchtopk}
//     sparsity_setting  str    e.g. "L1=0.4" / "K=8" / "K=16" / "K=32" / "theta=…" / "batchK=…"
//     mean_L0           f64    realized mean active features / token
//     alive_features    i64    # features with ≥ 1 firing across the eval set
//     dead_features     i64    dictionary_width − alive_features
//     fvu               f64    fraction of variance unexplained (recon quality)
//     max_abs_cos       f64    max |cos(decoder feature, r̂)| over alive features
//     top1_energy       f64    r̂ energy captured by top-1 aligned feature subspace
//     top5_energy       f64    r̂ energy captured by top-5 aligned feature subspace
//     top10_energy      f64    r̂ energy captured by top-10 aligned feature subspace
//     ablate_top10_ref  f64    adv refusal rate after ablating top-10 aligned subspace (n=20)
//     ablate_random10   f64    specificity control: 3-seed mean refusal under 10 random unit dirs
//     ablate_full_r     f64    PR #60 reference (full single direction r — should reproduce ≈0.00)
//     baseline_ref      f64    no-ablation refusal rate (should reproduce ≈ 0.95 on the same n=20)
//
//   The FIRST ROW IS THE CYCLE-20 ReLU L1 BASELINE, copied verbatim from
//   .verdicts/sandbox/m5_safety_sae_decomposition.txt (NOT re-trained — lever-
//   isolation attributes against the prior frozen baseline). Provenance for
//   that row's values: see the cycle-20 verdict §"RAW STDOUT" block.
//
//   No adversarial prompt strings, no generation completions, no per-prompt
//   labels are written to the TSV (cx_hf_safety_private). Only the aggregate
//   scalars above.
//
// ============================================================================
// HONEST FEASIBILITY NOTE — Hexa lacks a native SAE training surface
// ============================================================================
//   Per feedback_hexa_only_authoring (2026-05-23), this harness is `.hexa`
//   only — no new `.py` / `.sh` files are committed. But Hexa stdlib has no
//   native autodiff/SAE training primitives, so the SAE-train step MUST be
//   dispatched to an external library. The honest pattern (matching cycle-20's
//   bench/sandbox_m5_safety_sae_decomposition.hexa, which inlined its python
//   compute body in a `_payload()` string) is:
//
//     (i)   The .hexa harness owns the ORCHESTRATION shape: read the cached
//           L19 acts cache path, emit the per-family invocation plan, parse
//           the per-family alignment metrics back into the TSV.
//
//     (ii)  The actual SAE-train + alignment-compute python body is dispatched
//           via `pool on ubu-1 <python invocation>` against a pinned external
//           reference repo (see TODO_PIN_REFERENCE_REPO below). NO new .py
//           file lands in this repo — the python entry-point is either
//             (a) the external library's installed CLI, or
//             (b) a base64-shipped scratch script under the cycle-20
//                 ~/sandbox_probe/venv pin (reference_activation_capture_env),
//                 SAME pattern as cycle-20.
//
//     (iii) Adversarial text never crosses the wire: the ADV+BENIGN pool that
//           recomputes r is rebuilt in-process on ubu-1 (same as cycle-20),
//           and only NUMBERS come back into the TSV.
//
//   This skeleton commit STOPS at (i). The SAE-train dispatch is left as a
//   TODO marker — the next sub-cycle wires the actual `pool on ubu-1` calls
//   once the external-library pin lands. NO FIRE in this commit.
//
// ============================================================================
// TARGET HOST  (ubu-1 RTX 5070 12GB — same as cycle-20; 60k token-acts × 1536
// dims × fp32 = ~0.37 GB raw activations, fits in 12 GB next to a 6144-wide
// SAE and the 1.5B model loaded for r-recompute + ablation eval, per cycle-20).
// ============================================================================
//
// ============================================================================
// EXTERNAL REFERENCE REPO + COMMIT PIN  (honest "TBD pending lib pick")
// ============================================================================
//   Candidate libraries (cited at design time, NOT YET pinned — the
//   d_p2_runpod_compute_tier_runbook seed names this as the infra-prep step):
//
//     1. github.com/openai/sparse_autoencoder
//          — Gao, Goh, Heimersheim 2024 reference Top-K implementation.
//          — TODO_PIN_REFERENCE_REPO_COMMIT (pick a tagged commit, not main).
//
//     2. github.com/jbloomAus/SAELens
//          — Bloom et al community SAE library, supports Top-K / JumpReLU /
//            BatchTopK families behind a unified config — best single-library
//            surface for this 3-family head-to-head (most likely pick).
//          — TODO_PIN_REFERENCE_REPO_COMMIT (pick a tagged release commit).
//
//     3. github.com/EleutherAI/sae
//          — EleutherAI Top-K + JumpReLU implementation, smaller surface.
//          — TODO_PIN_REFERENCE_REPO_COMMIT.
//
//   DECISION (recorded for the next sub-cycle):
//     Default candidate = SAELens (covers all 3 families in one config space,
//     reduces install + venv-pin overhead vs running 3 separate libraries).
//     PIN COMMIT IS TBD until the next sub-cycle's `pool on ubu-1` install
//     dry-run lands (env-pin shape per reference_activation_capture_env).
//     Whichever library is chosen, its commit hash + install recipe lands in
//     the next sub-cycle's inbox/notes/p2-topk-sae-pin.md (per the
//     d_p2_runpod_compute_tier_runbook pattern for the larger scaleup).
//
// @no-lock-needed
// @resolver-bypass(reason="External SAE library dispatch on ubu-1 GPU + per-family alignment metric harvest; no atlas atoms touched. SKELETON ONLY — no fire.")

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Manifest of the lever-isolation plan, machine-readable inside the .hexa file.
// Three SAE families × their canonical sparsity settings, plus the frozen
// cycle-20 baseline row that the TSV will lead with.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn families() -> str {
    // The cycle-20 baseline is included at the head of the TSV (NOT re-trained;
    // copied from the cycle-20 verdict). The other rows are the lever-swap
    // candidates the next sub-cycle will dispatch.
    return "" +
        "relu_l1_baseline\tL1=0.4\t# CYCLE-20 frozen (read from .verdicts/sandbox/m5_safety_sae_decomposition.txt — NO re-train)\n" +
        "topk\tK=8\t# Gao-Goh-Heimersheim 2024 (arXiv 2406.04093) — sweep low\n" +
        "topk\tK=16\t# Gao-Goh-Heimersheim 2024 (arXiv 2406.04093) — sweep mid (~ cycle-20 L0 envelope)\n" +
        "topk\tK=32\t# Gao-Goh-Heimersheim 2024 (arXiv 2406.04093) — sweep high\n" +
        "jumprelu\ttheta=AUTO_TUNED_TO_MEAN_L0~16\t# Rajamanoharan et al 2024 (arXiv 2407.14435)\n" +
        "batchtopk\tbatchK=AUTO_TUNED_TO_MEAN_L0~16\t# Bussmann-Leask-Nanda 2024 (arXiv 2412.06410)\n"
}

// Frozen cycle-20 baseline row (verbatim copy from the verdict; do NOT edit
// these numbers without re-running cycle-20 — they are the comparison surface).
fn baseline_relu_l1_row() -> str {
    return "" +
        "relu_l1_baseline\tL1=0.4\t" +
        "3.57\t" +     // mean_L0
        "278\t" +      // alive_features
        "5866\t" +     // dead_features
        "0.0005\t" +   // fvu
        "0.0847\t" +   // max_abs_cos
        "0.0072\t" +   // top1_energy
        "0.0283\t" +   // top5_energy
        "0.0482\t" +   // top10_energy
        "0.9000\t" +   // ablate_top10_ref
        "0.8833\t" +   // ablate_random10
        "0.0000\t" +   // ablate_full_r (PR #60 reproduced)
        "0.9500\n"     // baseline_ref
}

// TSV header (column order matches the contract above and the cycle-20 row).
fn tsv_header() -> str {
    return "family\tsparsity_setting\tmean_L0\talive_features\tdead_features\tfvu\tmax_abs_cos\ttop1_energy\ttop5_energy\ttop10_energy\tablate_top10_ref\tablate_random10\tablate_full_r\tbaseline_ref\n"
}

// Path of the TSV emit (relative to repo root, per project_bench_sot — only the
// aggregate scalars land here; raw run logs stay on ubu-1 scratch SoT).
fn tsv_path() -> str {
    return ".verdicts/sandbox/p2_topk_sae_lever.tsv"
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SAE-train dispatch plan (the TODO surface the next sub-cycle wires).
// This function is the SoT for what gets shipped to ubu-1; the actual call is
// a `pool on ubu-1 <python invocation>` once the library pin lands.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
fn dispatch_plan() -> str {
    return "" +
        "# DISPATCH PLAN (SKELETON — wired by next sub-cycle, NO FIRE in this commit)\n" +
        "#\n" +
        "# host:      ubu-1 (aiden-B650M-K) RTX 5070 12GB  (cycle-20 substrate, $0)\n" +
        "# venv:      ~/sandbox_probe/venv  (clean pin — torch 2.12+cu130 transformers 4.51.3 numpy 1.26.4)\n" +
        "# acts cache: ~/sandbox_probe/l19_acts_cache.pt   (cycle-20 cached X = 59795 × 1536 fp32; reused)\n" +
        "#                                                  IF stale, recompute via the cycle-20 STEP 1 path.\n" +
        "# r vector:  recomputed in-process from PR #60's 20 adv + 20 benign matched pool (NO disk write)\n" +
        "#\n" +
        "# external library: TBD_PIN_SAE_LIB  (default candidate = SAELens, see header §EXTERNAL REFERENCE REPO)\n" +
        "# external commit:  TODO_PIN_REFERENCE_REPO_COMMIT  (resolved by next sub-cycle)\n" +
        "#\n" +
        "# TODO (next sub-cycle):\n" +
        "#   pool on ubu-1 'cd ~/sandbox_probe && venv/bin/python -m <TBD_PIN_SAE_LIB> \\\n" +
        "#       --acts l19_acts_cache.pt --width 6144 --epochs 40 \\\n" +
        "#       --family topk --k 8 --emit-tsv-row /tmp/p2_row_topk_k8.tsv'\n" +
        "#   (then repeat for K=16, K=32, jumprelu, batchtopk; concatenate the 5 rows\n" +
        "#    below the baseline row and write to .verdicts/sandbox/p2_topk_sae_lever.tsv)\n"
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// main — prints the orchestration manifest (families × the dispatch plan) so a
// caller can review the lever-isolation shape before the next sub-cycle wires
// the actual pool dispatch. NO FIRE: no pool call, no SAE train, no TSV write
// in this commit.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
fn main() {
    println("# bench/sandbox_p2_topk_sae_lever.hexa — LEVER-ISOLATION skeleton (cycle-22)")
    println("# tape: .discoveries/sandbox-p2-prod-sae.tape :: d_p2_top_k_sae_family")
    println("# baseline: .verdicts/sandbox/m5_safety_sae_decomposition.txt (cycle-20 ReLU L1)")
    println("# emit:    " + tsv_path())
    println("")
    println("# Families × sparsity settings:")
    println(families())
    println("# TSV header (frozen schema):")
    println(tsv_header())
    println("# Frozen cycle-20 baseline row (NOT re-trained):")
    println(baseline_relu_l1_row())
    println(dispatch_plan())
    println("# END SKELETON — next sub-cycle wires the pool dispatch + writes the TSV.")
}
